# MoonLight build-time options.
#
# These exist so the IDF Component Manager's dependency `rules` (in
# main/idf_component.yml) can branch on them: a rule's `if:` clause can test a
# Kconfig symbol, but only one that is *declared* (an undeclared symbol raises
# MissingKconfigError and fails the solve), so a plain sdkconfig.defaults line is
# not enough: the option must be declared here too.

menu "MoonLight"

    config MM_P4_WIFI
        bool "ESP32-P4: include WiFi via the on-board ESP32-C6 (esp_hosted)"
        default n
        help
            Pulls the esp_wifi_remote + esp_hosted managed components, which
            present the P4's companion ESP32-C6 radio through the standard
            esp_wifi_* API over SDIO. The P4 has no native radio.

            OFF by default so the Ethernet-only P4 firmware (esp32p4-eth) does
            NOT compile or boot esp_hosted, which self-initializes at boot
            and claims the default netif, which breaks the EMAC Ethernet DHCP
            path on an eth-only build (link up but no IP). Only the WiFi build
            (esp32p4-eth-wifi) sets this, via sdkconfig.defaults.esp32p4-eth-wifi.

    config MM_HLS
        bool "HLS streaming via the hardware H.264 encoder (ESP32-P4 only)"
        # Constrain the VALUE, not the visibility. `depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4` would hide the
        # symbol on every other target, and idf_component.yml gates the esp_h264 dependency on
        # $CONFIG{MM_HLS}: the solver cannot read a symbol that is not visible, so every non-P4
        # build failed with "MM_HLS ... not found in any Kconfig file". A range-style guard keeps
        # the symbol readable everywhere while making y impossible off the P4.
        default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default n
        help
            Pulls the esp_h264 managed component and compiles the platform HLS
            encoder (hardware H.264 + MPEG-TS muxer + PSRAM segment ring) behind
            the same HlsDriver the desktop uses. Only the P4 has the encoder
            silicon; other chips keep this off and build nothing.

    config MM_USB_MIDI_HOST
        bool "USB MIDI host on the chip's own USB port"
        default n
        help
            Pulls the espressif/usb managed component and compiles the USB MIDI
            host, so a class-compliant MIDI desk plugged into the board drives the
            MIDI service with no computer. Set by the S3 images with flash to spare
            (n8r8, n16r8); the 4 MB S3-Zero leaves it off.

    config MM_AUDIO_CODEC
        bool "Audio codecs in front of a microphone (ES8311, ES7210)"
        default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32S31
        default n
        help
            Pulls the esp_codec_dev managed component and compiles the codec
            bring-up, for the boards whose microphone sits behind an ES8311 or
            ES7210. On for the chips whose boards carry one (the S31 CoreBoard,
            P4 boards) and set by the Waveshare ESP32-S3-RGB-Matrix's image; a
            board with an I2S microphone such as the INMP441 needs no codec.

    config MM_SD_CARD
        bool "microSD card support (SDMMC or SPI)"
        default y
        help
            Compiles the FatFs SD card backend, on every chip with an SD host and
            more than 4 MB of flash: the 4 MB layout's fragment sets it off.

    config MM_FSEQ_ZSTD
        bool "zstd-compressed FSEQ playback"
        default n
        help
            Pulls a zstd decoder so FseqPlayerEffect can play FSEQ v2 files whose
            compression blocks are zstd (type 1), not just zlib. OFF by default
            everywhere: the decoder adds about 43KB of flash (measured on an
            esp32p4rev1-eth build) and real heap per open block, far above the
            zlib path's zero-flash-cost ROM call. Worth it on a board with
            flash and PSRAM to spare, such as the ESP32-P4; a board already
            tight on heap should leave this off.

endmenu
